# Could a planned economy work?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What was the socialist calculation debate between Mises, Lange and Hayek? Could computers solve the planning problem today?"
next: {"title":"What happened when they tried planning?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/59-what-happened-when-they-tried-planning.cast"}

## Mises's challenge
sources:
    - id: mises1920
      title: Economic Calculation in the Socialist Commonwealth (Die Wirtschaftsrechnung im sozialistischen Gemeinwesen)
      authors: Ludwig von Mises
      year: 1920
      finding: Without private ownership and markets for the means of production there are no prices for them, so no rational economic calculation is possible.

    icon office size 110 x 400 y 560 icon_look picture of "office building" hidden true
    text q1 "one office plans\nthe whole economy?" x 400 y 410 font_size 40 hidden true
    text q2 "if the planners are\nclever + honest" #8f887c x 400 y 250 font_size 32 hidden true
    mark q2_x around q2 kind strike hidden true
    portrait mises x 840 y 470 width 200 of "Ludwig von Mises" cameo true hidden true
    text y1920 "1920" #8a5fa8 cites ["mises1920"] x 840 y 250 font_size 36 hidden true
    box n_steel "Steel hull" #2f6b8f width 260 height 90 radius 12 font_size 34 in row hidden true
    text qmark "?" x 500 y 595 font_size 72 hidden true
    box n_timber "Timber hull" #b5482e width 260 height 90 radius 12 font_size 34 in row hidden true
    row row gap 240 fit.h 110 fit.w 820 fit.x 90 fit.y 540 hidden true
    text s_in "20 t steel\n900 h work" #2f6b8f x 250 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    text t_in "35 m³ timber\n1,400 h work" #b5482e x 750 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    text p_steel "$1,000 / t" #8f887c x 250 y 395 font_size 28 hidden true
    text p_wage "work: $40 / h" #8f887c x 500 y 395 font_size 28 hidden true
    text p_timber "$300 / m³" #8f887c x 750 y 395 font_size 28 hidden true
    text s_tot "$56,000" #2f6b8f x 250 y 320 font_size 44 hidden true
    text t_tot "$66,500" #b5482e x 750 y 320 font_size 44 hidden true
    mark s_ring around s_tot kind circle hidden true
    mark ps_x around p_steel s_tot kind cross hidden true
    text nosale "state owns mill + yard:\nno sale, no price" #b5482e x 500 y 230 font_size 30 hidden true
    text blind "no market → no price → choosing blind" x 500 y 230 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Could a planned economy work?"

Could one office run a whole economy, deciding what gets made, how much, and by whom?
    draw office q1

    pause 0.3

B: Most people's first answer: maybe, if the planners were clever and honest enough.
    draw q2

In 1920 the Austrian economist Ludwig von Mises said no. Not even saint (@draw q2_x@)s with perfect intentions could plan rationally.
    draw mises y1920

To see why, give Harbourtown a planner. The boatyard needs a new (@draw n_steel qmark n_timber parallel true@) hull, and there are two ways to build it.
    erase mises y1920 office q1 q2 q2_x

B: Steel: say twenty tonnes of steel and nine hundred hours of work. Or timber (@draw t_in@): thirty-five cubic metres of wood and fourteen hundred hours.
    draw s_in

    quiz "Which hull costs the town less?" judge false pool false
        right: There's no way to tell yet. Listen to the units.
        * Steel
        * Timber
        * Can't tell yet

Tonnes, cubic metres and hours: three different units, and no way to add them up.
    highlight s_in t_in effect underline

B: Prices turn them into one unit. Say steel at a thousand dollars a  (@draw p_steel@)tonne, timber at three hundre (@draw p_timber@)d, and work at forty dol (@draw p_wage@)lars an hour.

Now steel comes  (@draw s_tot@)to fifty-six thousand dollars, and timbe (@draw t_tot@)r to sixty-six and a half. Steel wins, because its price tells yo (@draw s_ring@)u what else that steel could have built.

    erase s_ring

B: But in Mises's socialism the state owns the steel mill and the boatyar (@draw ps_x@)d. The steel is never so (@draw nosale@)ld, so it has no price.

    highlight qmark effect pulse

That was his claim: without markets for machines, metal  (@draw blind@)and land, there are no prices for them, and without prices the planner chooses blind.
    erase nosale

    pause 0.5

## Lange's trial and error
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Steel (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      label: 'D: yards'' orders'
    supply:
      label: 'S: mills, P = MC'
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: false
    price_line:
      level: 35
      label: board's price
      show_gap: true
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 40
      w: 720
      h: 640
sources:
    - id: lange36
      title: On the Economic Theory of Socialism
      authors: Oskar Lange
      year: 1936
      finding: A Central Planning Board can set prices by trial and error, raising them where there is a shortage and lowering them where there is a surplus, while managers produce where price equals marginal cost; Lange suggested a statue of Mises in the board's hall for raising the problem.
    - id: barone08
      title: The Ministry of Production in the Collectivist State
      authors: Enrico Barone
      year: 1908
      finding: A collectivist ministry would have to solve the same system of equilibrium equations as a competitive market.

    portrait lange x 850 y 470 width 200 of "Oskar Lange" cameo true hidden true
    text statue "\"a statue of Mises\nin the board's hall\"" #8a5fa8 cites ["lange36"] x 850 y 250 font_size 28 hidden true
    text rule "managers' rule:\nP = MC" #2f6b8f x 850 y 520 font_size 30 hidden true
    text won "1930s verdict:\nLange won?" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mb cites ["barone08"] work [{"tex":"Z(P) = D(P) - S(P)"},{"tex":"P_{next} = P + k \\cdot Z(P)"},{"tex":"Z = 0:\\ P = MC"},{"tex":"n\\ \\text{goods} \\to n\\ \\text{equations}"}] x 380 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Lange's trial and error"

So the missing piece was a price. In 1936 the Polish economist Oskar Lange answered: then let the planners set one.
    draw axes lange

B: He even joked that socialists should put a statue of Mises in the planning board's great hall, for posing the problem so sharply.
    draw statue

Lange's board announces a price for steel. Each mill m (@draw supply_curve label_S@)anager follows one rule: produce until on (@draw rule@)e more tonne costs as much as that price.
    erase lange statue

B: And the boatyards order what they want at that price.
    draw demand_curve label_D

Say the board guesses too low. The yards orde (@draw gap_guide_qd gap_guide_qs label_Qd label_Qs@)r more steel than the mills make (@draw gap_arrow label_gap@): a shortage.
    draw price_line label_price

    keep price_line

B: So the board raises the price, the way a market would, but by decree.
    animate price_line.level 65 duration 3

Too high, and steel piles up unsold.
    highlight label_gap

So lower it again, step by step, until the shortage and the pil (@erase price_line_ghost gap_arrow label_gap gap_guide_qd gap_guide_qs label_Qd label_Qs@)e are bo (@draw equilibrium_point label_Pstar ends@)th gone.
    animate price_line.level 50 duration 3

Try being the board: drag its price and find the level where nothing is left over and nobody goes short.
    explore

B: Trial and error. The board never needs to know the curves, only whether there's a queue or a pile.
    highlight price_line

    erase rule

On paper, Lange had a market's prices without a market's owners, and many economists of his day thought he had won.
    draw won

    fade axes supply_curve label_S demand_curve label_D price_line label_price equilibrium_point label_Pstar label_E won to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq won

Call the excess demand Z: what the yards order minus what the mills make, at price P.
    draw mb_box mb_line_1

B: The board's rule: next month's price is this month's plus a little times Z. A queue raises it, a pile lowers it.
    draw mb_line_2

It only stops where Z is zero, with every manager producing where price equals marginal cost.
    draw mb_line_3

B: With n goods that's n such equations at once, which the Italian economist Enrico Barone had written down in 1908.
    draw mb_line_4

    erase mb

@test

    erase mathq won

    fade axes supply_curve label_S demand_curve label_D price_line label_price equilibrium_point label_Pstar label_E won to 1

    pause 0.3

## Hayek's two objections
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Steel (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      label: D
    supply:
      label: S
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: false
    price_line:
      level: 50
      label: board's price
      show_gap: true
    demand_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: 'D′: after the storm'
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: 'S′: reported cost'
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 40
      w: 720
      h: 640
sources:
    - id: hayek45
      title: The Use of Knowledge in Society
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1945
      finding: The knowledge an economy runs on is 'knowledge of the particular circumstances of time and place', dispersed among many people and never given to a single mind; prices pass it on.
    - id: hayek40
      title: 'Socialist Calculation: The Competitive ''Solution'''
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1940
      finding: 'Critique of Lange: prices fixed by a central authority would adjust too slowly to constantly changing conditions, and managers'' costs and performance could not be checked.'
    - id: kornai
      title: Economics of Shortage
      authors: János Kornai
      year: 1980
      finding: 'Socialist firms face a ''soft budget constraint'': losses are covered by the state, so costs are not disciplined.'

    text hayek "Hayek's reply" #8a5fa8 cites ["hayek40"] x 870 y 600 font_size 34 hidden true
    text millions "millions of prices,\none meeting a month" x 870 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    text quote "\"the particular\ncircumstances of\ntime and place\"\n— Hayek, 1945" #8a5fa8 cites ["hayek45"] x 870 y 410 font_size 28 hidden true
    text onlyhe "only the manager\nknows his MC" #2f6b8f x 870 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    text kornai_t "soft budget\nconstraint\n(Kornai)" #b5482e cites ["kornai"] x 870 y 260 font_size 28 hidden true
    text k1 "knowledge:\nnot on any desk" x 870 y 440 font_size 30 hidden true
    text k2 "incentives: nobody\npaid to tell the truth" x 870 y 280 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Hayek's two objections"

    hide shift_equilibrium_point shift_guide_lines label_E_shift label_P_shift label_Q_shift demand_shift_arrow supply_shift_arrow

Hayek's reply turned on where the information lives. His famous tin example, a price passing on news nobody had to explain, was his move in this very debate.
    draw axes supply_curve label_S demand_curve label_D equilibrium_point price_line label_price hayek

B: But Lange's board only watches for queues and piles. What's wrong with that?
    highlight price_line

The curves don't sit still. A storm wrec (@animate demand_shift.amount 25 duration 3@)ks half the fleet, and overnight every yard wants more steel.
    draw demand_shift_curve label_D_shift

    erase demand_curve label_D equilibrium_point label_E label_Pstar

B: In a market the price jumps that same week. Lange's boa (@draw gap_guide_qs gap_guide_qd gap_arrow label_gap@)rd still has last month's number on the wall.

And it isn't one price. It's millions, each waiting its turn at the board's next meeting.
    draw millions

    erase millions

Hayek's phrase for what the boar (@draw quote@)d lacks was knowledge of the particular circumstances of time and place: which mill has spare capacity, which order is urgent.

B: Knowledge scattered across millions of heads, and much of it nobody could write down.
    highlight quote effect box

    erase quote

Then there's the manager. The rule says produce until your cost e (@highlight supply_curve@)quals the price, but only he knows his cost.
    draw onlyhe

    quiz "The board sets each mill's target from the costs its manager reports, and pays a bonus for meeting the target. What cost does the manager report?" voice b
        right: Higher: a padded cost means a smaller target, and an easier bonus.
        wrong: An easier target is easier to beat: here, higher than his true cost.
        * His true cost
        + Higher than his true cost
        * Lower than his true cost

    draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift

B: So the board gets padded numbers, and a mill that loses money is simply topped up. The Hungarian economist János Kornai late (@draw kornai_t@)r called this the soft budget constraint.
    animate supply_shift.amount -15 duration 2.5

    erase onlyhe kornai_t hayek

So Mises asked where the prices come from. Lange said trial and error. Hayek said (@draw k1@) the errors are found too slowly, and the tr (@draw k2@)ials can't be trusted.

    pause 0.5

## Could a computer do it?
sources:
    - id: lange67
      title: The Computer and the Market
      authors: Oskar Lange
      year: 1967
      finding: '''Let us put the simultaneous equations on an electronic computer and we shall obtain the solution in less than a second.'' In Feinstein (ed.), Socialism, Capitalism and Economic Growth.'
    - id: kantorovich39
      title: Mathematical Methods of Organizing and Planning Production
      authors: Leonid Kantorovich
      year: 1939
      finding: The first linear-programming method for optimal production plans; its multipliers act as prices of scarce inputs. Nobel prize 1975, shared with Tjalling Koopmans.
    - id: cybersyn
      title: 'Cybernetic Revolutionaries: Technology and Politics in Allende''s Chile'
      authors: Eden Medina
      year: 2011
      finding: Project Cybersyn (1971–73), designed by Stafford Beer, linked factories by telex to an operations room in Santiago; it ended with the coup of September 1973.
    - id: nove
      title: The Economics of Feasible Socialism
      authors: Alec Nove
      year: 1983
      finding: Soviet sources counted about 12 million distinct products, if fully disaggregated.
    - id: cockshott
      title: Towards a New Socialism
      authors: W. Paul Cockshott and Allin Cottrell
      year: 1993
      finding: Argue that modern computers can solve the planning equations for a whole economy, because the input-output table is sparse.
    - id: walmart
      title: The People's Republic of Walmart
      authors: Leigh Phillips and Michal Rozworski
      year: 2019
      finding: Argue that giant firms such as Walmart and Amazon already plan vast flows of goods internally.
    - id: heilbroner
      title: After Communism
      authors: Robert Heilbroner
      year: 1990
      finding: 'The New Yorker: the socialist Heilbroner conceded that ''Mises was right'' about planning.'

    box n1 "1939\nKantorovich:\nshadow prices" #2f6b8f cites ["kantorovich39"] width 225 height 170 radius 10 font_size 30 in tl hidden true
    box n2 "1967\nLange: a computer,\nunder a second" #8a5fa8 cites ["lange67"] width 225 height 170 radius 10 font_size 30 in tl hidden true
    box n3 "1971–73\nCybersyn:\nfactories on telex" #b5482e cites ["cybersyn"] width 225 height 170 radius 10 font_size 30 in tl hidden true
    box n4 "today\nAmazon, Walmart,\nAI" #3d3833 cites ["walmart","cockshott"] width 225 height 170 radius 10 font_size 30 in tl hidden true
    row tl gap 25 fit.h 200 fit.w 960 fit.x 20 fit.y 400 hidden true
    text lq "\"we shall obtain the solution\nin less than a second\"" #8a5fa8 cites ["lange67"] x 500 y 280 font_size 38 hidden true
    scratch sc cites ["nove"] work ["12 million products",{"tex":"12{,}000{,}000^2 \\approx 144\\ \\text{trillion cells}"},"mostly zeros"] x 500 y 280 font_size 26 hidden true
    text nodata "the computer solves —\nbut who supplies the numbers?" x 500 y 270 font_size 40 hidden true
    text st1 "\"impossible in principle\": contested" #b5482e x 500 y 540 font_size 44 hidden true
    text st2 "a board replacing markets fails: mostly agreed" #2f6b8f cites ["heilbroner"] x 500 y 410 font_size 40 hidden true
    text st3 "computers + data change it: open" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 280 font_size 44 hidden true
    cards sortq bins ["Mises: no price","Hayek: too slow","Incentives"] check each hidden true
        * "Steel never sold" bin "Mises: no price"
        * "Storm overnight" bin "Hayek: too slow"
        Incentives: Manager pads costs, Losses topped up
    icon taxi size 120 x 500 y 470 icon_look picture of taxi hidden true
    text taxi_t "fares set from\nlast year's data" x 500 y 330 font_size 36 hidden true
    text close "on paper: nearly\nin practice: knowledge nobody holds,\nhonesty nobody is paid for" x 500 y 420 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Could a computer do it?"

So the board was too slow and its numbers padded. The obvious modern question: what about a computer?
    icon pc size 150 set twemoji x 500 y 470 icon_look picture of "desktop computer"

    erase pc

B: Lange said it himself in 1967: put the equations on an electronic com (@draw lq@)puter, and you'd have the solution in less than a second.
    draw n2

    erase lq

The maths was already Soviet. In 1939 Leonid Kantorovich showed how to plan production optimally, and his method threw out prices of its own: the worth of each scarce input.
    draw n1

B: And in Chile, from 1971 to 1973, Stafford Beer's Project Cybersyn wired factories to an operations room by telex, until the coup of 1973 ended it.
    draw n3

Then there's the size. One Soviet count put the economy at twelve million distinct products. A table linkin (@draw sc_line_2@)g each to every other has about a hundred and forty-four trillion cells.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

B: Most of them zero, though. Defenders like Paul Cockshott argue that today's computers handle problems that spars (@draw n4@)e, and Walmart and Amazon already plan vast flows of goods inside the firm.
    draw sc_line_3

    erase sc

But a computer solves the equations; it doesn't supply the numbers that go in: what people want tomorrow, a product nobody has thought of yet. And Amazon reads the market prices all around it.
    draw nodata

    erase nodata n1 n2 n3 n4

How sure are we? Mises's strong claim, that cal (@draw st1@)culation is impossible even in principle, is contested: Lange and Kantorovich showed prices can be computed.

B: That no central board can replace the knowledge and ince (@draw st2@)ntives of markets across a whole economy is mostly agreed. Even the socialist Robert Heilbroner wrote in 1990 that Mises was right.

Whether big data and AI could change that is open.
    draw st3

    pause 0.5

    erase st1 st2 st3

    draw sortq

    ask "Whose objection is each case: Mises's, Hayek's, or an incentive problem? Sort the cards." on sortq store srt stage own voice b
        right: All {srt} right: steel never sold is Mises's point, the storm is Hayek's, padding and top-ups are incentives.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total} on the first try. Steel never sold has no price: Mises. A storm outruns the board: Hayek. Padding and top-ups: incentives.

    erase sortq

    draw taxi taxi_t

    quiz "A city sets every taxi fare by computer from last year's data. A big concert ends at midnight. What would Hayek say goes wrong?"
        right: The fare can't carry tonight's news: the knowledge of time and place never reaches last year's data.
        wrong: Ask what last year's data knows about tonight: here, the fare can't carry tonight's news.
        * The computer is too slow
        + The fare can't carry tonight's news of where riders are
        * Drivers would earn too much

    erase taxi taxi_t

So could a planned economy work? On pa (@draw close@)per, nearly. In practice it needs knowledge nobody holds, and honesty nobody is paid for.

B: What happened when whole countries tried it is a story of its own.
    highlight close effect box

    pause 0.5

## Where next
