      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:bak3yp'
    '|b||Trial and error. The board never needs to know the curves, only whether there''s a queue or a pile.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||On paper, Lange had a market''s prices without a market''s owners, and many economists of his day thought he had won.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:ihquoz'
    '|b||Show the math, or skip it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2760
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:11ji0cd'
    '|a||Call the excess demand Z: what the yards order minus what the mills make, at price P.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6840
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||The board''s rule: next month''s price is this month''s plus a little times Z. A queue raises it, a pile lowers it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:iwm1gn'
    '|a||It only stops where Z is zero, with every manager producing where price equals marginal cost.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6240
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:38jeac'
    '|b||With n goods that''s n such equations at once, which the Italian economist Enrico Barone had written down in 1908.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8240
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1k310i1'
    '|a||Hayek''s reply turned on where the information lives. His famous tin example, a price passing on news nobody had to explain, was his move in this very debate.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11000
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||But Lange''s board only watches for queues and piles. What''s wrong with that?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4760
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:z2l6f9'
    '|a||The curves don''t sit still. A storm wrecks half the fleet, and overnight every yard wants more steel.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6320
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:kkdf2x'
    '|b||In a market the price jumps that same week. Lange''s board still has last month''s number on the wall.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6200
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1fo15zn'
    '|a||And it isn''t one price. It''s millions, each waiting its turn at the board''s next meeting.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5760
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:xfaulw'
    '|a||Hayek''s phrase for what the board lacks was knowledge of the particular circumstances of time and place: which mill has spare capacity, which order is urgent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10480
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:uw9je1'
    '|b||Knowledge scattered across millions of heads, and much of it nobody could write down.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:109tze9'
    '|a||Then there''s the manager. The rule says produce until your cost equals the price, but only he knows his cost.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:dmex2y'
    '|b||The board sets each mill''s target from the costs its manager reports, and pays a bonus for meeting the target. What cost does the manager report?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9560
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1r5jhsy'
    '|b||Higher: a padded cost means a smaller target, and an easier bonus.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||So the board gets padded numbers, and a mill that loses money is simply topped up. The Hungarian economist János Kornai later called this the soft budget constraint.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9720
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||So Mises asked where the prices come from. Lange said trial and error. Hayek said the errors are found too slowly, and the trials can''t be trusted.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:17jfmtc'
    '|a||So the board was too slow and its numbers padded. The obvious modern question: what about a computer?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7680
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Lange said it himself in 1967: put the equations on an electronic computer, and you''d have the solution in less than a second.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:a7kuif'
    '|a||The maths was already Soviet. In 1939 Leonid Kantorovich showed how to plan production optimally, and his method threw out prices of its own: the worth of each scarce input.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1ggkbn8'
    '|b||And in Chile, from 1971 to 1973, Stafford Beer''s Project Cybersyn wired factories to an operations room by telex, until the coup of 1973 ended it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11400
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:flc7e2'
    '|a||Then there''s the size. One Soviet count put the economy at twelve million distinct products. A table linking each to every other has about a hundred and forty-four trillion cells.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11680
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:14ddr7o'
    '|b||Most of them zero, though. Defenders like Paul Cockshott argue that today''s computers handle problems that sparse, and Walmart and Amazon already plan vast flows of goods inside the firm.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11200
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:nu9ef6'
    '|a||But a computer solves the equations; it doesn''t supply the numbers that go in: what people want tomorrow, a product nobody has thought of yet. And Amazon reads the market prices all around it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:q1mehr'
    '|a||How sure are we? Mises''s strong claim, that calculation is impossible even in principle, is contested: Lange and Kantorovich showed prices can be computed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:17adwii'
    '|b||That no central board can replace the knowledge and incentives of markets across a whole economy is mostly agreed. Even the socialist Robert Heilbroner wrote in 1990 that Mises was right.':
